# Benchmarks, 2026-11-15: cig against Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust

<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->

The 25 programs of [`bench/compute`](../bench/compute/), each written once in CigScript and once in Lua, Python and JavaScript (eight of them in Rust too), timed side by side on one machine. cig is measured as it is released: cig 1.24.0, and the programs cigcompile 0.5.0 builds from the same scripts. The others: Lua 5.4.9, LuaJIT 2.1, CPython 3.13.5, Node 22.23.5 and Rust 1.100.0.

Every measured number below is made from the samples in [`bench/results/2026-11-15/`](../bench/results/2026-11-15/) by [`bench/compare/report.py`](../bench/compare/report.py), and made again by [`bench/compare/check.py`](../bench/compare/check.py), a second program written apart from the first to [the same definitions](../bench/compare/SPEC.md). The two agree on every number and every character of this file: none was typed by hand.

## In short

- **Compiled, cig runs the 21 compute programs in {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}} of CPython 3.13's time**, {{inverse.compute.stage-b.python}} times as fast: a program `cigcompile build --stage b` makes runs its script's bytecode on cig's VM, and was faster than CPython on {{faster.compute.stage-b.python}} of the {{n.compute.stage-b.python}} programs, level on {{level.compute.stage-b.python}} and slower on {{slower.compute.stage-b.python}}. Against Node 22 it takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.node}} of the time; Node takes {{median.hello.node}} ms just to start, and most of these programs finish soon after.
- **The VM takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of the interpreter's time** ({{inverse.compute.stage-b.cig}} times as fast), faster on all {{n.compute.stage-b.cig}}. `cig run`, the tree-walking interpreter a script runs on today, takes {{geomean.compute.cig.python}} times CPython's time and {{geomean.compute.cig.node}} times Node's.
- **Lua is faster.** A compiled cig program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time, {{geomean.compute.stage-b.luajit-off}} times LuaJIT's interpreter's and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.luajit}} times LuaJIT's with its compiler on; and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.rust}} times Rust's, on the {{n.compute.stage-b.rust}} programs Rust has. The VM work to close the gap to Lua is [#243](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/issues/243).
- **cig starts fast**: `hello` takes {{median.hello.cig}} ms with `cig run` and {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms as a compiled program, where CPython takes {{median.hello.python}} ms and Node {{median.hello.node}} ms. Lua, LuaJIT and Rust start in about a millisecond.
- **This run found that cig slows down as its run history grows** ([#252](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/issues/252)): every run reads the whole run index first. `hello` with `cig run` took {{median.pass1.hello.cig}} ms over the first pass, while cig's history grew from {{history.first.pass1}} to {{history.last.pass1}} runs, and {{median.pass2.hello.cig}} ms over the second ({{history.first.pass2}} to {{history.last.pass2}}). Every cig time here comes from that growing history, so cig's times, its start-up most of all, are slower than a new install's would be.

## Against each language

A cell is cig's time divided by the other language's: the geometric mean, over the programs both have, of the ratio of their median times. Under 1, cig took less time. In brackets: how many of those programs cig was faster on, level with (within 3%) and slower on; the three add up to the programs the cell covers.

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **`cig run`** is cig's tree-walking interpreter, as `cig run script.cig` runs a script.
- **A stage `a` program** is what `cigcompile build` makes by default: cig itself with the script inside it, run by the same interpreter.
- **A stage `b` program** (`cigcompile build --stage b`) runs the script's bytecode on cig's VM.

These are the {{programs.compute}} programs that are not start-up tests; start-up has [its own section](#start-up).

